WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.706
Joining two tables is the most common thing a database does.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:11.412
The textbook way is a double for loop, and it dies on real data because it reeds the inner table once per outer row.

00:00:11.412 --> 00:00:16.118
Here is the disk-aware fix.

00:00:16.118 --> 00:00:19.654
Start on disk.

00:00:19.654 --> 00:00:23.190
The smaller table R is one page.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.726
The bigger table S is four pages, reed in order.

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:30.262
The whole trick is on the right.

00:00:30.262 --> 00:00:33.798
Load R into RAM once and keep it there for the entire join.

00:00:33.798 --> 00:00:37.334
Then stream S past one page at a time.

00:00:37.334 --> 00:00:40.870
Each S page drops into a single slot, gets probed against the R block, then is thrown away for the next one.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.407
Evaluate the join predicate on every pair.

00:00:44.407 --> 00:00:47.943
Emit the matches.

00:00:47.943 --> 00:00:51.479
Four S pages, two matches each, eight joined rows.

00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:55.015
R is reed once, S is reed once.

00:00:55.015 --> 00:00:58.551
P of R plus P of S, plus OUT.

00:00:58.551 --> 00:01:02.087
That best case only holds when R fits in B pages.

00:01:02.087 --> 00:01:05.623
If R is bigger, each block of R triggers another full scan of S, so always put the smaller table outer.

00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:08.549
And in code, just two loops.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:11.474
Hold a block of R in RAM, scan all of S against it, and emit the matches.

00:01:11.474 --> 00:01:14.400
Any predicate works.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.051
Now put numbers on it.

00:01:17.051 --> 00:01:19.703
Find all listens for each song.

00:01:19.703 --> 00:01:22.354
P of Songs is one hundred pages.

00:01:22.354 --> 00:01:25.005
P of Listens is ten thousand.

00:01:25.005 --> 00:01:27.656
Buffer of ten.

00:01:27.656 --> 00:01:30.308
Reed Songs once, one hundred I Os.

00:01:30.308 --> 00:01:32.959
That is ten blocks.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:35.610
Scan Listens once per block, one hundred thousand I Os.

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.261
Roughly one hundred thousand reeds, plus OUT.

00:01:38.261 --> 00:01:40.913
A row-by-row loop would scan Listens millions of times.

00:01:40.913 --> 00:01:43.564
Buffering alone makes it orders of magnitude cheaper.

00:01:43.564 --> 00:01:47.697
Block nested loop has a superpower.

00:01:47.697 --> 00:01:51.830
It handles any join condition.

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.963
Equality, range, similarity, distance.

00:01:55.963 --> 00:02:00.097
The double loop evaluates whatever predicate you hand it, so it will happily join on distance of audio vector less than zero point two.

00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:04.230
Every other join algorithm only does equality.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.363
When the join is not an equality, this is often the only option.

00:02:08.363 --> 00:02:11.468
So when does it win.

00:02:11.468 --> 00:02:14.573
Non-equality joins, always.

00:02:14.573 --> 00:02:17.678
One table small, reed it once and scan the big one a few times.

00:02:17.678 --> 00:02:20.782
Big buffer, few blocks, few scans.

00:02:20.782 --> 00:02:23.887
No indexes anywhere.

00:02:23.887 --> 00:02:26.992
Avoid it when both tables are huge and the join is a simple equality.

00:02:26.992 --> 00:02:30.097
Then the inner scan dominates and the cost goes quadratic.

00:02:30.097 --> 00:02:34.172
Block nested loop is the workhorse for small-times-big joins and for any non-equality predicate.

00:02:34.172 --> 00:02:38.247
But for a simple equality on two huge tables, the rescan kills you.

00:02:38.247 --> 00:02:42.322
Hash partitioning turns that quadratic cost linear.

00:02:42.322 --> 00:02:46.397
Hash partition join next.
